# Checking Project Decisions

## Overview

Every project here already decided its palette, fonts, easings, durations, copy language and what
must not change. Agents keep breaking those decisions because they never read them. **Read the
decisions before the first edit, prove you respected them after the last one.**

Violating the letter of a recorded decision is violating its spirit. "Close enough" is a new
decision nobody approved.

## The gate (all four steps, every time)

1. **Find the decisions.** Look the project up in `projects.md` (this folder). Unknown repo: search
   it for `DESIGN.md STACK.md CASE.md FINDINGS*.md DESIGN-PASS.md AGENTS.md RULES.md tokens.css`
   and read every hit. **Newest dated decision wins**; the token file in code beats prose.
2. **Write "Decisions I will respect"** before editing, 5-10 lines: mode (invent / preserve /
   recreate, one only), token file path, fonts, easings and durations, accent rule, copy language,
   the project's do-not-change items, the test command. No list, no edits.
3. **Build.** A value that is not a token (color, duration, easing, radius, size) means stop: reuse
   an existing token, or record a new decision (step 4). Brand decisions stay in their project;
   craft rules in `craft.md` apply everywhere.
4. **Prove it, then record it.** Report the `craft.md` audit as PASS / FAIL lines plus the
   project's own tests and screenshots. **Untested = FAIL: not tested.** Any new decision is
   appended, dated, with its why, to the project's decision file named in `projects.md`.

## Rationalizations

| Excuse | Reality |
|---|---|
| "It's a small tweak" | Small tweaks with raw values are how every audit found 20 drift findings |
| "The reference looks better" | A reference gives a mechanism, never a brand override. Mode decides |
| "This token is close enough" | Close enough is a new value. Reuse the exact token or record it |
| "I'll write the decision later" | Later never happens; the next agent reverts your choice |
| "The test selector was wrong, so I removed the assertion" | Update the selector. Never delete or weaken an assertion |
| "CASE.md says the accent is X" | Check newer dated sections and the token file first |

## Red flags: stop and go back to step 1

Raw hex, `rgba()`, `ms` or `cubic-bezier` in a component file · a fourth easing · a new font ·
restyling something the task did not name · "done" with no screenshot you opened · an empty
"Decisions I will respect" list.
# Rule: Design modes, handoff audit, polish order

**Applies to:** every UI task, before the first edit (mode) and before claiming done (audit).
Source: Framer AI's skill list and its audit of a real page, 2026-10-10. Only the parts this
factory did not already have; everything else lives in `anti-slop.md` and `verification.md`.

## 1. Pick ONE mode before touching anything

Every UI task runs in exactly one mode. Write it in the brief (or `STACK.md` for a new client).
Mixing modes is the main way delegates wreck a page: asked to fix spacing, they also "improve"
the colors.

| Mode | Use when | Never |
|---|---|---|
| **invent** | New site, new page or section, or the owner asked for a new theme or vibe | Use it on work that should match what exists |
| **preserve** | Editing, fixing or extending an existing page. The default when in doubt | Change tokens, fonts, palette, radii or motion that already exist. Reuse the page's own styles and components first; add a new value only when nothing existing fits, and say which |
| **recreate** | The owner attached a reference and asked to match or copy its look or structure | Use it when the image is content to place or loose inspiration. Copy the mechanism, never the files (`framer-references.md`) |

A task that needs two modes is two tasks.

Skill per mode: **invent** → `landing-page-design` · **preserve** → `redesign-existing-projects` ·
**recreate** → `framer-references.md`. All three end with `tastemaker` (`motion-and-taste.md`).

## 2. Handoff audit: additions to `verification.md`

Report each line as **PASS** or **FAIL**. **Untested is FAIL**, written as "FAIL: not tested",
never left out. Split confirmed defects from untested items.

- **Fold:** the product (the main offer, the demo, the menu) and the primary CTA are visible
  without scrolling at 375×812 and 1440×900. Decoration above them that pushes them down fails.
- **Scroll budget:** at 375px, a visitor reaches the offer, price and contact in **10 scroll
  gestures or fewer**. A guided "presentation" that needs ~50 scrolls fails, however pretty: on a
  phone it is hard to navigate and nobody books (IG @juandavidsierraa, 2026-10-24).
- **Width sweep:** besides the 375 and 1440 shots, check 320, 768 and 1024. Middle widths are
  where two-column layouts break. Nothing clips, overlaps or scrolls sideways.
- **Touch targets:** buttons at least 48px high, inputs and other controls at least 44px.
- **Inputs at 16px or larger.** Below 16px, iPhone Safari zooms the page when the field is tapped.
  Labels and notes no smaller than 14px.
- **Heading levels:** one `h1`; each section heading is `h2`; item titles inside a section
  (steps, cards) are `h3`, not styled paragraphs.
- **No fixed heights on anything that holds text.** Fixed heights are for media only; text boxes
  grow with their content, or the next copy edit clips.
- **Embeds and widgets use the page font.** A calculator, form or map embed with its own default
  font (Inter, system) is a fail.
- **Closing CTA:** the page repeats the primary action before the footer.
- **Share assets:** a favicon and a 1200×630 `og:image` exist and render.
- **Every button and anchor reaches a real destination.** Click each one.

## 3. "Make it premium" means this order

1. **Structure:** responsive layouts work at every width.
2. **Hierarchy:** the product is the biggest thing on the first screen; decoration shrinks.
3. **Copy:** sharpen the explanation. Keep a good headline; rewrite vague subtext.
4. **Consistency:** every one-off value becomes a token or a shared style.
5. **Motion polish:** within `motion-references.md`.
6. **Launch checks:** section 2 above, then `verification.md`.

Premium means nothing broken and everything consistent. Do not add effects to reach it.
Never add testimonials, client logos or claims without real material from the owner.
# Rule: Verification — evidence, not adjectives

**Applies to:** every completion claim in this factory. There is no file you can touch that
exempts you from this.

## The rule

> "It looks good", "should work", "is ready" are not completion claims.
> A file on disk, a passing command, or a screenshot is.

Never write **done · fixed · working · ready · listo** without naming the artifact that proves it.
The correct shape is:

> `pnpm build` clean · `pnpm typecheck` clean · screenshot at
> `clients/restaurante1/evidence/home-375.png`

## Gates by stack

| Gate | astro-static | nextjs-app | html-single |
|---|:--:|:--:|:--:|
| `pnpm build` clean | ● | ● | — |
| `pnpm typecheck` clean | ● | ● | — |
| `pnpm lint` clean | ● | ● | — |
| `pnpm test` green | if logic exists | ● | — |
| Screenshot @ 375px + 1440px | ● | ● | ● |
| Opens from `file://` | — | — | ● |
| Asset budget verified (`ls -lh`) | ● | ● | ● |
| Local SEO JSON-LD present | ● | ● | ● |

`pnpm typecheck` and `pnpm lint` are also enforced by `.claude/hooks/quality-gate.sh`, which
refuses to let the session stop while they are red. **Screenshots are not enforced by a hook** —
a Stop hook cannot start a dev server safely. That gate is on you.

## Capturing evidence

```bash
cd clients/<slug>
pnpm dev &                 # note the PID
pnpm exec playwright screenshot --viewport-size=375,812 \
  http://localhost:4321/ evidence/home-375.png
pnpm exec playwright screenshot --viewport-size=1440,900 \
  http://localhost:4321/ evidence/home-1440.png
kill %1
ls -lh evidence/
```

Evidence lives in `clients/<slug>/evidence/`. One screenshot per page, per breakpoint. Overwrite
freely — the latest state is what matters, not the history.

## DOM-count evidence — screenshots prove rendering, counts prove content

A screenshot proves the server responded, not that the right content is on it. Every evidence
run also asserts counts from the DOM and fails on mismatch:

- Rows/items rendered vs the content source (`menu.json` item count, not a hardcoded number).
- Map/address anchors present once per page (count `wa.me` / Maps endpoint anchors, not the
  JSON-LD string).
- Column/panel counts the layout balancer produced, so a panel split across two columns
  cannot pass silently.
- Zero console errors and zero images that did not decode — any one fails the run.

Name the report beside the shots (`evidence/<page>-report.json`) and the tool that produced
it (`tools/<page>-shots.mjs` against `pnpm preview`), so the next run is one command.

## Pre-done audit — run before claiming done

Every row below was a real gap found on a shipped client build. Check each before the gates:

- **Smooth-scroll anchors:** if Lenis (or any virtual scroll) is in play, the skip link and
  every in-page anchor still work (`anchors: true` or equivalent). Virtual scroll swallows
  anchors by default.
- **Focus visible:** every interactive element (logo link, nav links, full-width rows) has a
  real `focus-visible` style. The UA ring as fallback is a gap.
- **Contrast of the dimmest text:** measure the lowest-opacity text pair on the page
  (e.g. `text-text/50` on surface = 3.48:1, fails; `/60` = 4.86:1, passes). The mobile-first
  reader sees exactly the failing one.
- **Sticky offset:** `scroll-margin-top` on every anchor target under a sticky bar, so the
  no-JS path does not land the panel heading behind the bar.
- **Reduced data:** `prefers-reduced-data: reduce` is honored wherever heavy frames load.
  Firing all desktop frames as `new Image()` regardless is a gap.
- **`dist/` weight audit:** no unreferenced assets ship (`grep` refs in `src/` vs files in
  `public/`). Superseded originals and replaced posters are deleted, not left in `dist/`.
- **Screen-reader labels on price tables:** a heading row hidden with `aria-hidden` needs an
  `aria-label` per price cell, or the reader hears two numbers with no idea which is which.
- **JSON-LD image:** `LocalBusiness` carries `image`; interior pages carry `BreadcrumbList`.
- **Dead tokens:** every token in `tokens.css` is referenced by at least one rule. A declared,
  documented, unreferenced token is removed.

## Looking at the screenshot is part of the gate

A saved screenshot nobody opened proves the server responded, not that the page is correct. **Read
the image back** and check: does the text render, is the layout intact at 375px, is contrast real
against the background, did a font fail to load.

## Honest reporting

- If a gate fails, say so and show the output. A red build reported as green costs more than a
  red build reported as red.
- If a gate was skipped, say which and why. Silence reads as a pass.
- If something is partially done, name exactly what is missing. "Mostly working" is not a status.

## Premium scorecard — UI work is not done on a clean build alone

Order: `avoid-ai-design` scan (0 P0, hook-enforced) → `tastemaker` (0 CR craft-floor fails) →
this side-by-side against **one named real reference** from `CASE.md`'s reference research,
both at 1440 and 375. Each line PASS / FAIL, untested = FAIL:

| Check | Pass line |
|---|---|
| Real assets | Every hero/product image is the client's or commissioned; no stock, no unlabeled AI image |
| Signature move | Exactly one scroll/motion moment that only this client would have; the rest is calm |
| Type | Primary face chosen for the subject (not Inter/Geist/Poppins unless brand); ≤2 families |
| Performance | Lighthouse mobile LCP ≤ 2.5s, CLS ≤ 0.1; animation on transform/opacity only |
| Reduced motion | `prefers-reduced-motion` shows the final state with no motion |
| Reference gap | One line: what the reference does that this page still does not |
# Product

register: brand

## Product purpose
Personal portfolio of a software engineering student (UCC, Pasto, Colombia), graded for the Interfaces course and used as a CV link on LinkedIn/GitHub. Shows four class projects (desktop, mobile, smartwatch, video game) plus other work.

## Users
- Recruiters and hiring managers (LatAm and remote), scanning on a laptop or phone in under a minute, deciding whether to open the code.
- The Interfaces professor, grading against a checklist: nav always visible, hero with real photo and CV, about, projects with GitHub + live links, 3+ testimonials, experience, contact form validated with regex, footer with computed year, one extra section, strong primary color (not black/white), subtle and slow motion, no templates, no custom cursor, no skill bars.

## Brand and tone
Confident, precise, warm. Backend + AI integration engineer who cares about interfaces. Copy in English, short, specific, no em dashes.
Palette C "Cobalt & coral": cobalt is the only action color, coral is a fill accent.
Type: Bricolage Grotesque (display), Geist (body).

## References
eathungrytiger.com (pinned scroll story, big type), alimdesigner.com (3D corridor of project frames, pill nav, counter), belgradearbor.rs (shaped photo masks, word reveals), cartier "Le Choeur des Pierres" (sapphire chapters).

## Anti-references
Downloaded templates, purple gradients, glass on everything, skill bars with percentages, avatars instead of a real photo, all-black pages, generic three-card feature grids.

## Strategic principles
The projects are the product: they get the strongest motion and the most space. Motion is scroll-driven and reversible. Everything else stays quiet.
